60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,Option C seems the most sustainable. It uses the midwifery capacity Midori mentioned and avoids the high costs of new floor space.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,Option A is the only logical choice. My research showed that call-back trials actually reduced readmissions. We should formalize that success.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,I'm very concerned about Option A. Our ward nurses are already struggling with vacancies. I cannot support adding more to their load.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Option B sounds scary! A ""dedicated space"" means more rooms and more parking. We already can't park cars after eleven!","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Option C is lovely, but I worry about a ""joint"" delivery. If the two departments don't agree on the budget, it might vanish in a year.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, Option A involves home visits. I found that visits away from the hospital draw no tariff. It would be a financial drain.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, you are calculating the cost of the visit, not the cost of the readmission. A readmitted patient is far more expensive than a home visit.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, even if it saves money, who does the work? If it's the ward nurses, they will burn out. Option C is much safer for the staff.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Jonathan, I agree with you on C! It's scheduled consultations, which means we can manage the booking system better than a whole new unit.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, you are so right to think of the nurses. But @Jonathan, is Option D too reliant on those outside surgeries? I fear the paperwork.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, Option D would be a nightmare for the budget and the data agreements. I believe Option C is the most balanced choice.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Midori, if we use a dedicated team rather than ward nurses, the burnout issue is solved. The data proves the patient benefit is real.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, ""dedicated teams"" often end up being the same people on a different roster. I still feel Option C is the most harmonious choice.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, but Option A still needs to be booked! If we don't have a way to track those visits, they'll just disappear from the records.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"I think Option C is a wonderful way to support new families, provided we make sure both departments are truly committed to the long haul.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
